# Every function the `munsu` binary cannot reach, and why it is allowed to stay.
#
# `.github/scripts/deadcode.sh check` compares this file against
# `deadcode ./cmd/munsu` in both directions: unreachable in the tree but missing
# here is red, and listed here but reachable (or gone) is red too. So a new
# function that nothing in the binary can call cannot land quietly, and a line
# here cannot outlive the function it waives.
#
# This is a ratchet. It is only supposed to get shorter. Adding a line is
# allowed and sometimes right; doing it without a reason anyone would accept is
# how the file turns into a graveyard, and the reason column is self-declared,
# so reading the diff is what catches that -- not the script.
#
# Columns are tab-separated: file <TAB> function <TAB> reason
#
# No line numbers on purpose: they are not part of an entry's identity, and
# including them would rewrite this file on every unrelated edit above a
# function. Run `.github/scripts/deadcode.sh list` for the current keys.
#
# The set is the union across every GOOS in .github/build-tags.manifest, so a
# GOOS-gated file is covered even though only one build ever compiles it. That
# is why the linux and darwin halves of a `_linux.go`/`_darwin.go` pair both
# appear below, and why this file reads the same on a laptop and on the runner.

# --- Known open bugs, NOT accepted debt -------------------------------------
# A guard that exists, is complete, and cannot fire because nothing in the
# binary calls it. These are holes, not debt: the code was written to refuse
# something and the refusal can never happen. They are listed here only so the
# lane can go green; `deadcode.sh check` reprints every one as a warning on
# every single run, and each line names the issue that owns the verdict --
# wire it up or delete it. A line leaves this section with that issue, not
# before, and nobody may quietly demote one to accepted debt.
#
# `announce_open_bugs` runs on every `check`, so every line below is reprinted
# as a warning until its issue closes. An empty section is the intended resting
# state; it is empty right now: the last line (BEO-117, the agent-readiness
# predicate) left with the fix that made the function reachable from the binary.

# --- Not findings: GOOS-union artifacts --------------------------------------
# The set is the union across every GOOS, so a function reachable on one
# platform and unreachable on another lands in the union. These two are live
# code on the platforms that call them; there is nothing to burn down and no
# issue to open. Intersecting the platforms instead would be the wrong fix --
# it would hide a genuinely dead function in any GOOS-gated file.
#
# A platform-gated file whose function is unreachable on the very platform that
# compiles it is NOT this case. Those are ordinary debt and stay below.
internal/fleet/process_args.go	splitProcessCommand	GOOS-union artifact: live on darwin via process_inventory_darwin.go; the linux and windows builds have no caller for it
internal/fleet/process_args.go	writerKindForArgs	GOOS-union artifact: live on linux and darwin via process_inventory_{linux,darwin}.go; only the windows build cannot reach it

# --- Accepted debt, imported as the baseline --------------------------------
# What `deadcode ./cmd/munsu` reported on b7536f6, the first time it was ever
# run here, minus the ones triaged away since. They are pre-existing debt made
# visible, not debt this lane created. Triaging them one by one -- wire up,
# delete, or waive with a real reason -- is the burn-down in ADR-0009 (BEO-67).
#
# Every line below carries the same marker, and that marker means "nobody has
# looked at this yet". It is not a justification and must not be reused on a
# new entry -- a new entry gets a real reason or it does not go in.
internal/config/project_overlay.go	StoreProjectOverlay	triaged as test-only, kept in production code on purpose. Production reads project overlays (fleet ResolveProjectSnapshot -> config.LoadProjectOverlay) but has never written one: the document is operator-authored and a missing document is a valid empty overlay by design. Its only writers are tests in internal/config AND internal/fleet, so it cannot move into a config _test.go the way SetDeliveryCrashHookForTest did.
internal/domain/identity.go	NewResourceID	burn-down(BEO-63): pre-existing, imported with the baseline, not yet triaged
internal/domain/identity.go	ParseCanonical	burn-down(BEO-63): pre-existing, imported with the baseline, not yet triaged
internal/domain/lifecycle.go	NextGeneration	burn-down(BEO-63): pre-existing, imported with the baseline, not yet triaged
internal/domain/lifecycle.go	RetryAfter	burn-down(BEO-63): pre-existing, imported with the baseline, not yet triaged
internal/domain/lifecycle.go	ValidateUplinkTransition	burn-down(BEO-63): pre-existing, imported with the baseline, not yet triaged
internal/domain/operation.go	Replay	burn-down(BEO-63): pre-existing, imported with the baseline, not yet triaged
internal/domain/operation.go	ReusedWithDifferentIntent	burn-down(BEO-63): pre-existing, imported with the baseline, not yet triaged
internal/fleet/task_handoff_transaction.go	SetHandoffCrashHookForTest	burn-down(BEO-63): triaged as test-only, kept in production code on purpose. Its only consumer is internal/cli/captain_handoff_test.go, a different package, so the hook cannot move into a fleet _test.go the way SetDeliveryCrashHookForTest did. The binary never reaches it; the crash-boundary variable it sets stays package-private.
internal/home/wake_lease.go	ReclaimExpiredLeases	triaged as a test-only reclaim seam used across home and orchestrator tests; ClaimWakes would immediately claim re-enqueued wakes and hide re-enqueue counts, resolved-event suppression, and lease-file removal
